{"title":"TS EAMCET","category":"Engineering","bank":"ts_eamcet","subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","count":6,"questions":[{"id":111619,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"Using mathematical induction, the numbers $a_n$ 's are defined by,\n$a_0=1, a_{n+1}=3 n^2+n+a_n,(n \\geq 0) .$\nThen, $a_n$ is equal to","options":["$n^3+n^2+1$","$n^3-n^2+1$","$n^3-n^2$","$n^3+n^2$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\text { Given, } a_0=1, a_{n+1}=3 n^2+n+a_n \\\\\n& \\Rightarrow \\quad a_1=3(0)+0+a_0=1 \\\\\n& \\text { and } \\quad a_2=3(1)^2+1+a_1=3+1+1=5\n\\end{aligned}$\nFrom option (b),\n$\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text { Let } & P(n)=n^3-n^2+1 \\\\\n\\therefore & P(0)=0-0+1=1=a_0 \\\\\n\\text { and } & P(1)=1^3-1^2+1=1=a_1 \\\\\n& P(2)=(2)^3-(2)^2+1=5=a_2\n\\end{array}$\nHence, option (b) is correct.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"TS EAMCET 2009","_marksId":"65b6b47104bd81e61a27c4ed"},{"id":111620,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"$\\sum_{k=1}^{2 n+1}(-1)^{k-1} \\cdot k^2$ equals to","options":["$(n-1)(2 n-1)$","$(n+1)(2 n+1)$","$(n+1)(2 n-1)$","$(n-1)(2 n+1)$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} & \\sum_{k=1}^{2 n+1}(-1)^{n-1} k^2 \\\\ & =\\left[1^2-2^2+3^2-4^2+\\ldots-(2 n)^2+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\left[1^2+3^2+5^2+\\ldots+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & -\\left[2^2+4^2+6^2+\\ldots+(2 n)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\left[1^2+2^2+3^2+4^2+\\ldots+(2 n+1)^2\\right] \\\\ & -2\\left[2^2+4^2+6^2+\\ldots+(2 n)^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\sum(2 n+1)^2-2 \\times 2^2\\left[1+2^2+3^2+\\ldots+n^2\\right] \\\\ & =\\frac{(2 n+1)(2 n+1+1)\\{2(2 n+1)+1\\}}{6}-\\sum n^2 \\\\ & {\\left[\\because \\sum n^2=\\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}\\right]} \\\\ & =\\frac{(2 n+1)(2 n+2)(4 n+3)}{6} \\\\ & -8 \\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6} \\\\ & =\\frac{(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}[2(4 n+3)-8 n] \\\\ & =\\frac{(n+1)(2 n+1)}{6}[8 n+6-8 n] \\\\ & =(n+1)(2 n+1) \\\\ & \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"TS EAMCET 2014","_marksId":"65b6b4d404bd81e61a27c832"},{"id":111621,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"The coefficient of $x^n$ in the expansion of $\\frac{1}{x^2-5 x+6}$ for $|x| \\lt  1$ is","options":["$\\frac{1}{2^{n-1}}-\\frac{1}{3^{n-1}}$","$\\frac{1}{2^{n+2}}-\\frac{1}{3^{n+2}}$","$\\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}-\\frac{1}{3^{n+1}}$","$\\frac{1}{2^n}-\\frac{1}{3^n}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"We have,<br />$\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x^2-5 x+6} & =\\frac{1}{(x-3)(x-2)} \\\\ \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{1}{x-2} \\\\ \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{-3}\\left(\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{x}{3}}\\right)-\\frac{1}{-2}\\left(\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{x}{2}}\\right) \\\\ \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{-1}-\\frac{1}{3}\\left(1-\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^{-1} \\\\ \\end{aligned}$<br />$\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\therefore \\text { Coefficient of } x^n & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^n-\\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^n \\\\ \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}-\\frac{1}{3^{n+1}} \\\\ \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"TS EAMCET 2015","_marksId":"65b6b4de04bd81e61a27c887"},{"id":111622,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"Among the following four statements, the statement which is not true, for all $n \\in N$ is","options":["$(2 n+7) \\lt (n+3)^2$","$1^2+2^2+\\ldots+n^2\\gt\\frac{n^3}{3}$","$3.5^{(2 \\mathrm{n}+1)}+2^{3 \\mathrm{n}+1}$ is divisible by 23","$2+7+12+\\ldots+(5 n-3)=\\frac{n(5 n-1)}{2}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"By option checking, take $n=1$\n(1) $9 \\lt 16$, True.\n(2) $1\\gt\\frac{1}{3}$, True\n(3) $3 \\cdot 5^3+2^4=375+16=391$, not divisible by 23, False\n(4) For $n=1,2=2$, True.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"TS EAMCET 2024 (10 May Shift 1)","_marksId":"677b9ddc72c536f56b16acee"},{"id":111623,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"For all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is divisible by","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>11</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>For all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> is divisible by</p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced></math> be the statement given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced><mo>:</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced><mo>:</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>35</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p><p>Divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math> is true</p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mi>m</mi></mfenced></math> be true</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mi>m</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mi>i</mi></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mi>m</mi></mfenced></math> is true</p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> be true</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>:</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>From equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>i</mi></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>.</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>.</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo>.</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>.</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math> is true</p><p>Hence, it is divisible by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Easy","source":"TS EAMCET 2021 (04 Aug Shift 2)","_marksId":"65b6b68104bd81e61a27d649"},{"id":111624,"subject":"Mathematics","chapter":"Mathematical Induction","exam":"Engineering","examName":"TS EAMCET","q":"For $n \\in N$, the largest positive integer that divides $81 n+20 n-1$ is $k$. If $S$ is the sum of all positive divisors of $k$ then $S-k=$","options":["117","130","115","127"],"answer":0,"solution":"$81^n+20 n-1 \\Rightarrow(1+80)^n+20 n-1\\begin{aligned}\n& =1+80 n+{ }^n C_2 \\times 80^2 \\times 1+\\ldots .+20 n-1 \\\\\n& =100 n+100 \\times 8^2 \\times{ }^n C_2+{ }^n C_3 80^3 \\ldots \\\\\n& =100\\left(n+{ }^n C_2 \\times 64 \\ldots .\\right) \\\\\n& k=100=2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\end{aligned}$<br/>Sum of all divisors is\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\left(1+2+2^2\\right) \\times(1+5+25)=7 \\times 31=217=S \\\\\n& S-K=217-100=117 .\n\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"TS EAMCET 2024 (09 May Shift 1)","_marksId":"677b9db272c536f56b16abae"}]}